ll ancestors up to root (since tree must be connected). So we need to decide which removable nodes to include to maximize utility subject to keeping non-removable nodes and at least one node for each required symbol.\n\nThis is a classic tree knapsack or maximum weight connected subgraph problem with mandatory nodes. Since tree is small (7 nodes), brute force over subsets is possible. But we need a general solution? The fixture shows only 7 nodes per case, but the problem may have larger trees. However, we can implement a DP or recursion.\n\nGiven the lack of explicit description of \"replacement candidates\", I'll assume the simple case: we can only delete removable nodes; we cannot add nodes. The final tree must be a connected subtree containing root and all non-removable nodes. Also must contain at least one node for each required symbol. The removable nodes can be optionally kept. The goal is to maximize total utility of kept nodes.\n\nBut note: The problem statement says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". \"Replacement\" might mean we can replace a parent node with its child? That would be like pruning a node and attaching its children to its grandparent. That could increase connectivity options. But that is more complex. However, looking at the fixture tree: root has three children: n1 (s1, non-removable), n2 (s2, removable), n3 (s0, non-removable). n2 has child n4 (s1, removable); n3 has child n5 (s2, non-removable); n1 has child n6 (s0, removable). So the tree is small. Required symbols: root, s1. We have non-removable nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. So we must keep those. Then we need to include at least one node with symbol s1. n1 is s1 and non-removable, so that requirement is already satisfied. So we can optionally keep or remove removable nodes n2, n4, n6. If we keep n2, we must keep its parent root (already kept), and its child n4? No, we can keep n2 but remove n4? But if we keep n2, we can still remove n4 because n4 is removable child. Ho