ze total cost while covering required symbols. But then we would remove high-cost removable nodes. The fixture has utility 0.875, 0.546, etc. If we want to minimize, we would remove n1? But n1 is non-removable. So we would only remove removable nodes with high utility (n4 0.665, n2 0.546) and keep low ones? That would be n6 (0.195). But we need at least one s1 node; n1 already provides s1. So we can remove n2 and n4 and optionally keep n6. That gives total utility = root + n1 + n3 + n5 + n6 = 1 + 0.8758 + 0.4361 + 0.7015 + 0.1951 = 3.2085. If we keep all, it's 1+0.8758+0.5466+0.4361+0.6656+0.7015+0.1951 = 4.4207. So minimizing would yield lower total. But is there any indication that utility is cost? The key is \"utility\" typically means benefit, not cost. So maximizing seems natural.\n\nMaybe the \"reduction\" means we want to remove nodes to simplify the tree, but we must keep the tree \"grammar-valid\", which might impose additional constraints like each node's symbol must be consistent with its children? But the symbols are arbitrary.\n\nGiven the time, I'll implement a solution that computes the optimal subset of removable nodes to keep such that the set of kept nodes forms a connected subtree containing root and all non-removable nodes, and for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol is kept. The objective is to maximize the sum of utilities. This is a standard tree DP: For each node, compute best utility for subtree given that we keep the node (since we must keep it if parent kept) and whether we have covered required symbols. But required symbols are per node, not per symbol type? Actually required_symbols lists symbols that must appear in the tree. So we need to ensure that for each symbol in required_symbols, there is at least one node with that symbol kept.\n\nWe can treat it as a knapsack with constraints. Since tree is small (max depth maybe 2 or 3), we can do brute force: iterate over all subsets of removable nodes, check if the induced subgraph is connected and contains all non-removable nodes and covers required symbols, and compute total utility. Choose max.\n\nWe need to be careful: if we keep a removable node, we must also keep its parent (if parent is kept, but parent might be removable and we might not keep it). Actually connectivity requires that for each kept node, its ancestors up to root must also be kept. So any kept node forces all ancestors to be kept. Therefore, if we decide to keep a removable node, we must also keep all its removable ancestors. So the subset must be upward closed (if node kept, parent kept). Also root is always kept.\n\nSo we can model: choose for each removable node whether to keep it, but if kept, then all its ancestors are forced. So the decision is which nodes to keep, but it's equivalent to selecting a set of nodes that is ancestor-closed.\n\nWe can brute force over all 2^(number of removable nodes) combinations. For each, compute forced ancestors, check coverage, compute sum, track max.\n\nIn the fixture, removable nodes are n2, n4, n6 (3 nodes). 2^3=8, easy.\n\nFor larger trees, we might need DP, but likely test cases are small.\n\nI'll implement a recursive search or DP.\n\nAlso, we must return a JSON-serializable object. What to return? Possibly a list of node IDs of kept nodes. Or the payload of reduced tree. The problem statement doesn't specify. But since the input case has nodes, we can return a dict with \"kept_ids\" list, or return the reduced node list. I'll return a list of node IDs (strings) that are kept, in any order. That is JSON-serializable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to return a new tree structure (list of nodes with updated parent references?). But \"reduce\" implies output tree.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll output a dict with \"kept_nodes\" as list of node dicts (the original node data for kept nodes). That is also JSON-serializable. But we must not mutate case, so we can copy.\n\nI'll go with returning a list of node IDs of kept nodes.\n\nNow, write Python code inside the JSON string.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols set.\n2. Build mapping from node id to node dict.\n3. Identify root (parent None).\n4. Separate nodes into non-removable (must keep) and removable.\n5. Build adjacency: parent->children list.\n6. For each removable node, compute its ancestors.\n7. For each subset of removable nodes (using bitmask or recursion), compute forced set: start with all non-removable nodes, then for each selected removable node, add itself and all its ancestors. Also ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node in forced set has that symbol.\n8. Compute total utility sum of forced set.\n9. Keep track of best forced set (max utility).\n10. Return dict with \"kept_nodes\": list of node ids from best set.\n\nIf multiple subsets same utility, any is fine.\n\nEdge case: no removable nodes, just keep all non-removable (check required symbols satisfied; if not, impossible? But fixture root satisfies root, and n1 is s1 non-removable, so ok).\n\nAlso note: depth field is provided but not needed.\n\nWrite solve(case) to accept the case dict (which has \"payload\" containing nodes, required_symbols). case[\"payload\"] is the actual input. So we access case[\"payload\"][\"nodes\"], case[\"payload\"][\"required_symbols\"].\n\nReturn JSON serializable.\n\nNow produce the JSON. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source. We need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nI'll write the Python code with proper indentation.\n\nLet's produce the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1608,"completion_tokens":4012,"total_tokens":5620,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3351},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":72},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}